# Documentation

## SoT

- **Notion** — product, domain, UX intent, durable decisions (Décisions DB; never ADR Markdown in Git).
- **Git** — code, HTTP contract, local persistence, active i18n conventions, contributor docs (`docs/**`).
- Cross-link Notion ↔ Git docs/README. Do **not** put Notion links or decision provenance in source comments.
- One normative truth → one SoT; elsewhere link only.

## Code comments (all languages)

- Professional **English**.
- Explain non-obvious behaviour or real technical constraints only.
- Prefer naming/structure over comments; no comments that restate the next line.
- Do **not** document project history, discarded alternatives, V1/V2/MVP framing, or “as decided in …”.
- Remove or rewrite comments that contradict current code; prefer no comment over an invented one.

## When to update docs

- Code-only, product truth unchanged → Git only.
- Product / domain / UX / architecture truth changes → update the Notion reference page.
- Durable trade-off → Décision row + SoT link + update reference.
- On close: fix false Play’Up status / README summaries when they no longer match reality.

## Public docs language

- `README.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `SECURITY.md`, contributor-facing `docs/**` → **English**.
- Pull request **titles and bodies** created or drafted for this repository → **English**, professional tone (clear, concise, no slang or casual asides). Align with `.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md` when filling a description.
- Product narratives in Notion may be French.
